    "product_id": "8433d56c-2145-4dab-a0bf-3b6052d1d1a9",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 211153\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n453 AM PDT Wed Oct 21 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Multiple rounds of rain and storms expected northwest of \n  Interstate 15 and in northwestern Arizona through Thursday.\n\n* The greatest risk of flash flooding and severe storms exists\n  over northwestern Arizona.\n  \n* Dry conditions and seasonable temperatures expected this weekend \n  and early next week. \n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...through Tuesday.\n\nLatest satellite imagery shows our system spinning off the CA\ncoast in the form of a closed low. As expected, scattered \nshowers and storms have developed across our northern/northwestern\nzones overnight in the left exit region of an upper-level jet \nstreak. This activity is forecast to continue through the day \nacross Inyo, Nye, and Esmeralda counties, though coverage and \nintensity will vary over time. A secondary area of convection is \nbeginning to form across Mohave, Lincoln, and northeastern Clark \ncounties this morning as the jet streak translates east and robust\nmoisture advection occurs. PWATs between 1-1.25\" should allow for\nfairly efficient rainfall within this convective regime, though \nquick storm motions mean any flash flooding will likely require \ntraining convection. Best chances of this are in northern Mohave \nCounty, where a Flash Flood Watch is in effect through Thursday \nafternoon. In addition to the flood threat, northwestern AZ will \nhave the risk of severe thunderstorms today, and to a lesser \nextent on Thursday. The SPC has given this area a marginal to \nslight risk of severe storms, including the potential for a \ntornado or two, damaging winds, and hail up to 2\" in diameter \ntoday. Anomalous low-level moisture and decent lapse rates will \nfoster CAPE values between 500-1500 J/kg. Hodograph curvature is \nlimited, but low-level wind shear is rather impressive for the \narea (25-35 knots in the lowest 3km based on forecast soundings). \nThese long, straight hodographs will favor splitting supercells, \nwith the left split carrying the greatest hail risk and the right \nsplit having the greatest tornado risk. However, abundant storm \ncoverage/interactions may complicate or limit the severe threat. \nThe severe risk may carry over into the overnight hours tonight if\nstorms manage to stay in our portion of AZ.\n\nThe primary vort max of the low/trough swings through on Thursday, \nbringing the best chance of widespread precipitation to our \nregion. PoPs on Thursday are 50% or more in most locations, though\nthe southern Mojave Desert remains the greatest area of \"bust\" \npotential with PoPs 10-40%. Precipitation chances largely exit the\nCWA Thursday night once the shortwave trough axis clears us, but \na secondary shortwave diving south will keep the larger trough \noverhead on Friday. This allows shower chances (20-40%) to linger,\nprimarily in the southern Great Basin. The trough finally pushes \neast on Saturday, although guidance has trended slower with this. \nThe weekend should be much drier, but if the trough continues to \nslow, low precipitation chances may linger in our eastern areas on\nSaturday. \n\nThe hydro/convective concerns are going to be the primary focus\nwith this system, but there are other aspects worth mentioning \nas well. Snow levels bottom out around 8kft, but most of the \nprecip will fall while they are 9kft+, thus limiting any winter \nimpacts to the higher elevations of the Sierra. Gusty winds will \nbe present as well with this storm, with widespread southerly \ngusts of 20-40 mph today and Thursday. Friday into Saturday, we \nsee a shift to gusty north winds as the system pulls away. \nTemperatures will be near normal today, dropping to below-normal \nvalues tomorrow through Saturday. Dry and seasonable conditions \nexpected Sunday through Tuesday as we see a lull in the storm \ntrack.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 12Z Forecast Package...Light\nwinds gradually veer to the east by mid-morning, increasing to \naround 8KT. By early to mid-afternoon, winds shift to the \nsoutheast, increasing to 10-15KT with gusts to around 20-25KT \nexpected thereafter through sunset. Winds become light and \nsomewhat variable overnight, increasing out of the southwest \ntoward the end of the forecast period. Clouds will be SCT-BKN \naround 8-12kft through the forecast period, with some improvement\nexpected this afternoon. While thunderstorm activity largely \nremains outside of the Las Vegas Valley, impacts to approaches can\nbe expected due to scattered to numerous thunderstorms across the\nMormon Mesa and Peach Springs Corridors this afternoon onward.\n\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast\nCalifornia...For the 12Z Forecast Package...Winds around 5-10KT\nthis morning will shift to the south and southeast by late \nmorning into early afternoon and become elevated, with gusts to \n20-30KT common through sunset. Isolated showers this morning will \ngive way to more widespread shower and thunderstorm development by\nthis afternoon, especially across the southern Great Basin, \nnorthwestern Arizona, and portions of southeastern California. \nConvection is largely expected to stay out of the vicinity of area\nterminals, with the exception of KBIH, where at least vicinity \nshowers and thunderstorms are expected through the forecast \nperiod. The best window for thunderstorm impacts at KBIH will be \nbetween around 21/20Z-22/02Z, with erratic gusty winds, lightning \nstrikes, and ceiling/visibility reductions due to rainfall \nexpected. Cloud bases will generally be at or above 10kft, but \nwill be as low as 6-8kft in the vicinity of convection.\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Woods\nAVIATION...Phillipson\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
